<p>A storm rolls across Bexar County, a pipe gives way during a hard freeze, or a kitchen fire fills the house with smoke. Within hours you are on the phone with your insurer, and within days an adjuster you have never met is walking through your home deciding what the damage is worth. <strong>Residential property claims in San Antonio</strong> can move fast at the start and then stall, especially after a large storm when thousands of neighbors are filing at the same time. As a San Antonio Public Adjuster, our job is to represent you, the homeowner, from the first inspection through the final payment, so the claim reflects the actual damage to your house.</p>

<h2>Residential Claims We Help San Antonio Homeowners With</h2>
<p>Most homeowners claims fall into a few broad categories. Many involve more than one, such as hail that damages a roof and then lets rain into the attic.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hail and roof damage.</strong> Bexar County hail season peaks in April and May. See <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">hail damage claims</a> and <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage insurance claims</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Wind and storm damage.</strong> Downbursts, lifted shingles, fallen trees, broken windows, and damaged fences. NOAA lists more than 300 thunderstorm wind reports in Bexar County.</li>
<li><strong>Water damage.</strong> Sudden plumbing failures, appliance leaks, slab leaks, and storm-driven rain. See <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Freeze damage.</strong> Burst pipes and ceiling collapses like those many San Antonio homes saw during Winter Storm Uri in February 2021. See <a href="/services/freeze-damage-claims/">freeze damage claims</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Fire and smoke.</strong> Structural fire, smoke and soot contamination, and water damage from firefighting, plus odor that can linger in HVAC systems and contents.</li>
<li><strong>Contents and additional living expenses.</strong> Personal property and the cost of living elsewhere while your home is repaired, when the policy provides it.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Why San Antonio Homes Present Unique Claim Issues</h2>
<p>San Antonio housing is varied, and that variety shows up in claims. A 1920s pier-and-beam home in Monte Vista, King William, or Tobin Hill has very different framing, plaster, and wood siding than a slab-on-grade tract home in Stone Oak or Converse. Common local materials include composition shingle roofs, standing-seam metal roofs on the Hill Country edge, and stucco, limestone, or brick veneer walls. Each material has its own repair methods, and an estimate that treats every house the same may miss real costs.</p>
<p>The weather adds its own complications. NOAA records show more than 600 hail reports in Bexar County since 1957, including the April 12, 2016 storm across the northwest side that was widely reported as one of the costliest hailstorms in Texas history. San Antonio also sits in "Flash Flood Alley" along the Balcones Escarpment, where heavy rain can turn a roof claim into a flood-versus-wind coverage question. Expansive clay soils can raise questions about whether cracks are storm-related or long-term settlement. You can look up past events near your address with our <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup</a>.</p>

