<h2>Understanding the Coverages in Your Homeowners Policy</h2>
<p>A complete residential claim looks beyond the obvious damage. Your declarations page lists each coverage, its limit, and your deductibles.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Coverage</th><th>What it generally includes</th><th>What to check</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Dwelling (Coverage A)</td><td>The house and attached structures</td><td>Replacement cost or actual cash value, roof payment schedules, wind/hail deductible</td></tr>
<tr><td>Other structures (Coverage B)</td><td>Fences, detached garages, sheds, carports</td><td>Separate limit, often a percentage of Coverage A</td></tr>
<tr><td>Personal property (Coverage C)</td><td>Furniture, clothing, electronics, appliances</td><td>Replacement cost vs. ACV, special limits on jewelry and similar items</td></tr>
<tr><td>Loss of use (Coverage D)</td><td>Additional living expenses if the home is unlivable</td><td>Time and dollar limits, receipts required</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ordinance or law</td><td>Increased costs to meet current building codes on covered repairs</td><td>Whether you have it and its limit</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Many Texas policies carry a separate wind/hail deductible that is a percentage of the Coverage A limit, which can be much larger than the all-other-perils deductible. The difference between replacement cost and actual cash value also matters; our article on <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value in Texas</a> explains how recoverable depreciation typically works.</p>

<h2>Our Residential Claim Process</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Free claim review.</strong> We talk through what happened, look at any letters or estimates you already have, and tell you honestly whether we think we can help.</li>
<li><strong>Policy review.</strong> We read the full policy, including endorsements, to identify the coverages, limits, deductibles, and exclusions that apply.</li>
<li><strong>Property inspection.</strong> We inspect the roof, exterior, attic, and interior, photographing and measuring the damage. Matterport 3D imaging can capture interior conditions before repairs begin.</li>
<li><strong>Estimate.</strong> We prepare a line-item estimate in Xactimate, the estimating software most insurers use, so the two estimates can be compared item by item.</li>
<li><strong>Presentation and communication.</strong> We submit the documentation, handle communication with the insurer's adjuster, and track responses and deadlines.</li>
<li><strong>Supplements and resolution.</strong> When hidden damage turns up during repairs, we document and submit it. We stay involved until the claim is resolved.</li>
</ol>

