<h2>Common Homeowners Claim Problems</h2>
<p>Some of the most frequent issues we see in residential claims include:</p>
<ul>
<li>A roof approved for repair when matching or the condition of the shingles may call for replacement of a slope or the whole roof.</li>
<li>Interior damage left out because it was not visible on the day of inspection.</li>
<li>Missing line items such as drip edge, starter course, ridge vents, flashing, gutters, or permits.</li>
<li>Depreciation applied in ways the homeowner does not understand.</li>
<li>Contents and additional living expenses never claimed at all.</li>
</ul>
<p>If your claim has already been paid but the amount will not cover repairs, see <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid insurance claims</a>. If it was denied, see <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied insurance claims</a>.</p>

<h2>What You Should Do Right Away</h2>
<ul>
<li>Report the claim promptly and write down the claim number.</li>
<li>Take reasonable steps to prevent further damage, such as tarping a roof or shutting off water at the meter, and keep receipts.</li>
<li>Photograph and video everything before cleanup, including wide shots and close-ups.</li>
<li>Do not throw away damaged materials until they have been documented.</li>
<li>Request a full certified copy of your policy.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> walks through each step.</p>

